    "q-336840": {
      "dom_id": "step-q336840",
      "name": "Question 15",
      "problem": "A $10\\,500\\,\\text{mL}$-capacity water tank initially holds $7\\,200\\,\\text{mL}$ of water containing $210\\,\\text{g}$ of dissolved contaminant. Clean water begins to flow into the tank at a rate of $90\\,\\text{mL/s},$ while the completely mixed water flows out at a rate of $60\\,\\text{mL/s}.$ Find, rounded to two decimal places, the amount of contaminant in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow.",
      "worked_solution": "Let $Q$ be the amount of contaminant in the tank at time $t.$ Then, the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in seconds. This is equal to the rate at which contaminant enters the tank minus the rate at which contaminant leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{g}/\\text{s}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which contaminant enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that clean water (i.e., water containing $0\\,\\text{g/mL}$ of contaminant) enters the tank at a rate of $90\\,\\text{mL/s}.$ So, the rate at which the *contaminant* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mL}})\\cdot (90\\,\\frac{\\text{mL}}{\\text{s}}) \\\\  & =(0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}})\\cdot (90\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}{\\text{s}}) \\\\  & =0\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{s}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which contaminant exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which contaminant leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of water in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $7\\,200\\,\\text{mL}$ of water; clean water enters at $90\\,\\text{mL/s},$ and mixed water leaves at $60\\,\\text{mL/s}.$\nAs a result, the amount of water in the tank after $t$ seconds is \n\n$$\nV(t)=7\\,200+90t-60t=30(240+t)\\,\\text{mL}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the tank is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of contaminant in the tank, the concentration of the water at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{30(240+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mL}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed water leaves at a rate of $60\\,\\text{mL/s},$ *contaminant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(60\\,\\frac{\\text{mL}}{\\text{s}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{30(240+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mL}}) \\\\  & =(60\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}{\\text{s}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{30(240+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{mL}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{240+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{s}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{2Q}{240+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}=-\\frac{2}{240+t}\\,Q.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt} & =-\\frac{2}{240+t} \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\frac{dQ}{dt}dt & =\\int (-\\frac{2}{240+t})\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{Q}\\cdot \\,dQ & =-2\\int \\frac{1}{240+t}\\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =-2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|240+t|)+C \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|Q| & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(240+t)^{-2}|+C \\\\ |Q| & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(240+t)^{-2}|+C} \\\\ Q(t) & =\\frac{A}{(240+t)^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $A=\\pm {e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $210\\,\\text{g}$ of contaminant. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=210,$ and solve for $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =210 \\\\ \\frac{A}{(240+0)^{2}} & =210 \\\\ A & =210(240)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =\\frac{210(240)^{2}}{(240+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =210{(\\frac{240}{240+t})}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the tank begins to overflow. The tank has a capacity of $10\\,500\\,\\text{mL},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=10\\,500$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =10\\,500 \\\\ 30(240+{t}_{o}) & =10\\,500 \\\\ 240+{t}_{o} & =350 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =110\\,\\text{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the amount of contaminant in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(110) & =210{(\\frac{240}{240+110})}^{2} \\\\  & =210{(\\frac{24}{35})}^{2} \\\\  & ≈98.74\\,\\text{g},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places.",
